Dropbear
========

Dropbear 2026.94, by Matt Johnston, the SSH server and client, from
https://matt.ucc.asn.au/dropbear/dropbear.html
(https://matt.ucc.asn.au/dropbear/releases/dropbear-2026.94.tar.bz2,
SHA-256 e098034a843699200c8c977a991fff73159735bf795d5f72ef672c41a6b1ae81;
the release publishes a signature, not a checksum). MIT licence, with
the other pieces' terms beside it: LICENSE. The bundled LibTomCrypt
1.18.2 and LibTomMath are in the public domain, or under the WTFPL for
LibTomCrypt, at the user's choice (libtomcrypt/LICENSE,
libtommath/LICENSE).

Only what Belka builds is here: src/'s C sources and headers, the
manual pages, LibTomCrypt's src/ and LibTomMath's sources, each with
its LICENSE and README. Not here: Dropbear's configure script and
Makefiles, its tests, fuzzers, Debian files and helper scripts, and
the libraries' own build files, demonstrations, tests and documents.

None of the sources is changed. Belka builds them without configure or
the Makefiles (mk/userland.mk), over Belka's own pieces in
usr/sbin/dropbear:

    config.h        in place of configure's, written for mlibc and the
                    compat library (whose crypt it declares)
    localoptions.h  Belka's choices over default_options.h

default_options_guard.h is made from default_options.h by the sed of
Dropbear's ifndef_wrapper.sh, in the build. LibTomCrypt's tables
(*tab.c) are not built alone: the files that use them include them.

The choices, from localoptions.h and config.h:

  - no privilege drop at authentication (DROPBEAR_SVR_DROP_PRIVS):
    it needs setresgid, which Belka has not, so the server is root
    until the session's fork, where it becomes the user; with it,
    Unix-domain socket forwarding is off
  - no X11 forwarding, SFTP server, re-execution (no fexecve), zlib,
    PAM, lastlog, or the old utmp and wtmp: sessions are in utmpx and
    wtmpx (/var/adm)
  - the seed from /dev/urandom (Belka has no getrandom)
  - the host keys in /etc/dropbear, the pid file /var/run/sshd.pid,
    and PATH as /etc/profile sets it

The programs are installed under OpenSSH's names: dropbear as
/usr/sbin/sshd, dbclient as /usr/bin/ssh, dropbearkey as
/usr/bin/ssh-keygen; scp is scp, and dropbearconvert keeps its name.
Their options are Dropbear's, not OpenSSH's: see their manual pages,
installed under both names. The client's own key is ~/.ssh/id_dropbear
unless -i names another; authorized keys and known hosts are in
~/.ssh/authorized_keys and ~/.ssh/known_hosts, as OpenSSH's.

sshd runs under inetd by default (/etc/inetd.conf: "sshd -i -g", no
root logins by password); /etc/rc.d/sshd makes the host keys at the
first boot and, with sshd_standalone=YES, runs it as a daemon instead.
